    "q-115798": {
      "dom_id": "step-q115798",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve for $w$ where $\\frac{4-w}{3w}+\\frac{2w-1}{2w}=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We note that $w=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $2w$ and $3w.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2w,3w)=6w.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6w,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-w}{3w}+\\frac{2w-1}{2w} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6w\\cdot (\\frac{4-w}{3w}+\\frac{2w-1}{2w}) & =6w\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ 6w\\cdot (\\frac{4-w}{3w})+6w\\cdot (\\frac{2w-1}{2w}) & =6w\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3w}\\cdot (\\frac{4-w}{\\cancel{3w}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{2w}\\cdot (\\frac{2w-1}{\\cancel{2w}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot w\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 2(4-w)+3(2w-1) & =4w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4-w)+3(2w-1) & =4w \\\\ 8-2w+6w-3 & =4w \\\\ 5 & =4w-4w \\\\ 5 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
